id: regexp/matching/b8-flat-sequence/0001
area: regexp
op: RegExpBuiltinExec, repeated inputs
in: str:"(?:a)" int:100000 str:"" str:"u" str:"a" int:100000 str:"" int:0
out: value: str:"lastIndex=0;index=0;length=1;0=100000"
class: divergence
divergence.broiler-js: row B8 as revised 2026-11-01 (owner's decision D-p5): a flat sequence of 100,000 (?:a) under u on 100,000 a; Broiler.JS answers, since its u patterns went to Broiler.Regex 0.1.0-preview.5 (a58d31d1), the whole input after about 4 s evaluated alone on the runs' host, a time that grows about as the square of the length, so a run beside other runs renders the runner's abort at its 5 s limit (each of the four first runs did), where the row recorded a process abort, a stack overflow in Matcher.ReadAt, under preview.4; the difference this field classifies is the runner's 5 s limit, not the row's class (a correction of Broiler.JS and of Broiler.Regex's preview.4, JSD-0081's R4, no CLR recursion in the matcher), which preview.5 already meets, since the process no longer aborts; the time is not attributed here, nothing yet shows that SJL-11.5, Broiler.JS's cut-over to Broiler.Regex, removes it, and its classification stays open for the owner; the vector so breaks SCHEMA.md section 7's premise that a runner's limit is far above any vector's time, which a later revision may restore by a smaller input
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-regexpbuiltinexec spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-compilepattern spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-compilesubpattern spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-updatemodifiers spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-getstringindex ref:regexpref@1
script: function $n(r, m) { var t = "lastIndex=" + r.lastIndex; if (m === null) return t + ";null"; t += ";index=" + m.index + ";length=" + m.length; for (var i = 0; i < m.length; i++) t += ";" + i + "=" + (m[i] === undefined ? "u" : m[i].length); return t; } var r = new RegExp("(?:a)".repeat(100000) + "", "u"); r.lastIndex = 0; $n(r, r.exec("a".repeat(100000) + ""))
note: correspondence row B8: a flat sequence of 100,000 terms under u: the whole input, with no recursion per term
since: 31

